## Intuition

The idea is to explore all possible subsequences of the array and check which ones add up to the given target `k`.
For every element, we have two choices: either include it in the subsequence or skip it. By trying both choices recursively, we can examine all valid subsequences and count the ones whose sum becomes exactly equal to `k`.

---

## Approach

We use recursion to traverse the array from left to right.

* If at any point the remaining sum `k` becomes `0`, it means we have found a valid subsequence, so we increase the count.
* If we reach the end of the array or the sum becomes negative, we stop exploring that path.
* At each index, we make two recursive calls:

  * One by including the current element in the sum.
  * Another by excluding the current element and moving forward.

This ensures that all possible subsequences are considered.

---

## Solution

```cpp
class Solution{
    public:
    void solve(vector<int>& nums, int k, int &count, int index) {
        if (k == 0) {
            count++;
            return;
        }

        if (index == nums.size() || k < 0) return;

        solve(nums, k - nums[index], count, index + 1); // include element
        solve(nums, k, count, index + 1);               // exclude element
    }

    int countSubsequenceWithTargetSum(vector<int>& nums, int k){
        int count = 0;
        solve(nums, k, count, 0);
        return count;
    }
};
```

---

